{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792777387,"nonce":"bAHcDm/LuZeOFVoplHR5SUZAWfI=","message":"Design: interpreter executes MIR with the native representation.\n\nWhy. Promotion has to continue a suspended interpreter frame in compiled code (no replay), and compiled code has to call interpreted functions. Both need the interpreter's state to be the native state. Today it is not:\n- registers are NaN-boxed tagged values (48-bit i64 payloads, function ids instead of addresses);\n- strings are interpreter-owned Rust Strings, rebuilt into fake HaxeString headers at each FFI call;\n- structs, unions and closures live in an interpreter object heap, not memory;\n- globals live in a private store, so values the interpreter writes are invisible to native code;\n- string/number builtins reimplement the runtime;\n- _setjmp is called through FFI, so its frame is dead before the try body runs (any throw longjmps into it);\n- MemCopy/MemSet operate on an unused 1MB buffer.\n\nInterpreter-first conformance baseline at 4e5089f0 (wrapper forcing --tier-start-interpreted true): pass 260, wrong 28, compile_fail 725, crash 79 out of 1092 scored. ~640 of the 725 are \"Cannot convert String(..) to i64\", ~50 are closure structs.\n\nRepresentation (mirrors the Cranelift lowering, which is what compiled code expects):\n- Register = raw 64-bit word, typed per register with a machine type (I8/I16/I32/I64/F32/F64) derived the way the Cranelift lowering derives value types. Operand coercions (sextend/uextend/ireduce/fcvt/fpromote) are resolved when the function is decoded, using the same rules as the native lowering (binop operation type, phi-edge coercion, call-argument coercion, C-extern promotion, return coercion).\n- String constant = call haxe_string_literal(ptr, len) on leaked bytes, as native code does.\n- Alloc / CreateStruct / CreateUnion / sret = per-frame slot memory at a stable address (native: stack slots).\n- FunctionRef / MakeClosure = malloc'd {fn_ptr, env} objects with the native env layout. fn_ptr is a real code address: the compiled function if there is one, otherwise a trampoline into the interpreter.\n- CallIndirect = load fn_ptr/env from the closure object; a trampoline target is entered directly, anything else is a native call with the call-site signature.\n- Globals = the runtime's slots (rayzor_global_slot), shared with compiled code.\n- Externs = the runtime symbol, called with the native signature; no reimplemented builtins. malloc/realloc/free = libc. The intrinsics the Cranelift lowering inlines (math rounding, Std.int, array length/get_ptr) are evaluated with the same semantics.\n\nTrampolines. Fixed 16-byte position-independent stubs in an RX region; each loads its own address and jumps to one assembly entry. The entry looks the stub up in a forwarding table and tail-jumps when the function has native code, otherwise saves the argument registers and calls a dispatcher that enters the interpreter. The table is ordinary memory, so installing native code never writes executable pages.\n\nNative calls from the interpreter go through one assembly helper that loads an argument block into registers and the stack, so arity is not limited to what transmuted fn types can express.\n\ntry/catch. _setjmp is executed by an assembly helper that calls setjmp in its own frame and then calls back into the interpreter to run the rest of the frame inside it. A longjmp returns to the helper with 1 and the interpreter continues the same frame on the catch path.\n\nOrder of work: representation + externs + strings + memory + globals + closures/trampolines + setjmp; then calling compiled functions directly; then OSR continuation of the suspended frame instead of replay (this issue's replay half); then presets (5df946a).","files":null}]}